@Temka193 The blog post [10000 Update() calls](https://blogs.unity3d.com/2015/12/23/1k-update-calls/) has some more information, but the key point is: Update/Awake/etc aren't `virtual` methods inherited and `override`n from `GameObject`; they're magic methods that the Unity engine calls when they exist. That's why they work when `private` and don't need the `sealed`, `virtual`, or `override` .
Unity's native code keeps a list of `MonoBehaviour`s which are subscribing to lifecycle events and then trampolines into the managed code to call the magic methods when appropriate. As that native->managed transition has cost, it's best to not include Unity event methods when they aren't needed.
